Urticaria
Rheumatology & Immunology
Intensely pruritic wheals, Blanching on pressure, Resolution within 24 hours

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-049
Reason for Visit: Sudden, explosive eruption of intensely itchy, raised red welts across the entire body, causing severe distress and an inability to sleep or function.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient experiencing a rapidly spreading, intensely pruritic total-body rash. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to evaluate the airway for impending angioedema (anaphylaxis risk), confirm the diagnosis of acute urticaria versus a dangerous systemic vasculitis or drug reaction, and provide immediate, high-dose antihistamine therapy, as the severe pruritus and anxiety were unmanageable at home.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My entire body is covered in these massive, raised red welts, the itching is driving me absolutely insane, and they keep moving around."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the sudden eruption of intensely pruritic, erythematous, and raised wheals over the last 6 hours. They note the fleeting nature of the individual wheals, describing how a massive welt on their thigh faded completely within 2 hours without leaving a scar, only to be replaced by new lesions on their arms and chest (rapid emergence of new lesions). The patient admits to intense scratching, which immediately exacerbates the development of new wheals (dermatographism). Denies any deep, non-pruritic swelling of the lips, eyelids, or tongue (angioedema), or any difficulty breathing. Reports worsening of symptoms in the evening. The patient started a new course of oral antibiotics (Penicillin) for a mild sinus infection yesterday. Denies fever, joint pain, or feeling systemically unwell.
Functional Status: Highly agitated, pacing, unable to sit still or sleep due to the severe, unrelenting pruritus.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute distress and sleep deprivation; risk of secondary skin infection from excoriation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is highly distressed, constantly scratching arms and legs, but speaking in full sentences without respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Dermatological: Widespread, varied-sized (1cm to 10cm), raised, erythematous, edematous plaques (wheals) with pale centers distributed across the trunk, extremities, and neck. The lesions completely blanch upon applied pressure. Firm stroking of unaffected skin produces a linear wheal within minutes (positive Darier's sign/dermatographism). No bruising, purpura, or target lesions noted. HEENT: Uvula is midline, airway is completely patent. Absolutely no swelling of the lips, eyelids, or tongue. Respiratory: Lungs clear, no wheeze or stridor. Cardiovascular: Heart sounds dual, no hypotension. The diagnostic tetrad is completely aligned: intensely pruritic wheals, blanching on pressure, and a clinical pattern of rapid resolution/migration within 24 hours, anticipating complete resolution of cutaneous lesions following administration of H1-antihistamines.
Environmental Observations: Patient has tried taking a cold shower with minimal lasting relief.
Medication Reconciliation: Took one dose of over-the-counter cetirizine (10mg) two hours ago with no effect. Recently commenced Penicillin.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Urticaria - Status: Exacerbated (Acute severe drug-induced flare)
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of respiratory compromise (no wheeze/stridor), no mucosal swelling (angioedema), and perfectly stable hemodynamics (no hypotension or presyncope).

Urticarial Vasculitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lesions are highly fleeting (resolving in hours), blanch completely, and leave no residual bruising or hyperpigmentation, whereas vasculitic lesions persist >24 hours and often burn rather than itch.

Erythema Multiforme - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of classic target lesions with three distinct color zones, and the rapid, migratory nature of the wheals.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe, total-body eruption of classic urticaria, highly likely triggered by the recent penicillin prescription. While not currently in anaphylaxis, the intensity of the pruritus is causing severe functional distress and agitation. Urgent after-hours assessment was necessary to physically examine the airway and hemodynamics to definitively rule out anaphylaxis, instruct the immediate cessation of the offending antibiotic, and prescribe an aggressive, up-titrated regimen of second-generation antihistamines combined with a short course of oral corticosteroids. Deferring care would have resulted in an agonizing, sleepless night, justifying the MBS 585-600 service.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately cease the offending Penicillin antibiotic. Initiate an aggressive antihistamine regimen: take up to four times the standard dose of a non-sedating H1-antihistamine (e.g., Cetirizine 40mg daily or Loratadine 40mg daily) in divided doses off-label, combined with an H2-blocker (e.g., Famotidine) for dual histamine blockade. Prescribe a short, sharp course of oral corticosteroids (e.g., Prednisolone 25mg for 3-5 days) to rapidly break the severe inflammatory cycle.
Positive Behavioral Change: Pruritus management: Apply wrapped ice packs directly to the most intensely itchy wheals instead of scratching; explaining that the intense cold physically overloads the local nerve endings, overriding the itch signal to the brain and preventing the physical release of more histamine caused by scratching. Environmental cooling: Sleep with a light sheet and a fan directly on the body; confirming that keeping the skin cool physically causes the blood vessels in the skin to constrict, actively reducing the swelling and redness of the wheals.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to assess rash resolution and prescribe an alternative, non-penicillin antibiotic for the original sinus infection.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately or call 000 if you develop any swelling of your lips, tongue, or throat, if you suddenly feel a tightness in your chest or have difficulty breathing, or if you feel dizzy and like you are going to faint (signs of anaphylaxis).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a severe, acute allergic rash, most likely triggered by your new antibiotic. We have ruled out a life-threatening allergic reaction (anaphylaxis) tonight, as your airway and blood pressure are perfectly stable.
Clinical Course: With the high-dose antihistamines and steroids, the intense itching and the rash should improve dramatically within 12 to 24 hours. You must completely avoid Penicillin-class antibiotics in the future.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not take another dose of the Penicillin. If you suddenly feel your throat tightening, your lips swelling, or if you feel faint or start wheezing, this indicates the reaction is worsening into a dangerous allergy, and you must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system has reacted violently to the antibiotic, dumping massive amounts of histamine into your skin. This chemical causes the blood vessels to leak fluid (the welts) and triggers the intense nerve itch. Scratching physically breaks open more histamine cells, making it worse. The massive dose of antihistamines will block these chemicals, and the cold packs will soothe the nerves.
Actionable Steps: Stop the Penicillin immediately. Take the prescribed high-dose antihistamines and steroids. Apply ice instead of scratching. Keep your bedroom very cool tonight.

